# Files in templates/ are copied into somebody's Linux build and executed there, so they must keep
# LF line endings in the working tree on every platform.
#
# Why this is not cosmetic: git on Windows checks files out with CRLF by default, which turns the
# shebang into "#!/bin/sh\r". A POSIX shell then reports "bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory", naming a path that plainly exists, which is one of the more time-consuming errors to
# read. AppRun and the bundle scripts are the files this would hit.
#
# The patterns are enumerated by type rather than written as templates/**, and the icon is marked
# binary explicitly. A blanket text rule over a directory containing a PNG corrupts the PNG: git
# strips CR bytes from it on commit, and the first sign of trouble is an icon that no longer
# decodes. That happened while writing this file, which is why the rule is narrow.
templates/AppRun text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

*.png binary
*.AppImage binary
